---
import ComparisonPageLayout from '../../layouts/ComparisonPageLayout.astro';

// Competitor facts checked against Warp's own docs, repo and blog on
// 2026-10-11. Research notes: docs/research/2026-10-11-competitive-landscape-refresh.md
const comparison = {
  competitor: {
    name: 'Warp',
    icon: '🚀',
    tagline: 'Agentic development environment (open-source client)',
  },
  tldr: {
    title: 'TL;DR',
    points: [
      'Warp open-sourced its terminal client in April 2026 under AGPL-3.0, the same license as Caro. Its agent harness, server and Warp Drive stay closed and hosted.',
      'Every Warp AI request goes through Warp\'s servers, even with your own API key. Local models need a public HTTPS tunnel. Caro runs local models with no network at all.',
      'The Warp app replaces your terminal. The Warp Agent CLI (August 2026) runs in any terminal, but needs a Warp account. Caro needs no account.',
      'Warp asks before agent commands that match an editable regex denylist. Caro checks every command against 52+ curated patterns, rates the risk, and blocks critical commands.',
      'Warp is a full agentic workspace: agents, Drive, teams and cloud agents. Caro does one job: natural language to a safe shell command.',
    ]
  },
  headToHead: [
    { feature: 'Open source', caro: true, competitor: 'partial', winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'AI works fully offline', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Local models without a tunnel', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'No account needed for AI', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Curated dangerous-command patterns', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Risk level for each command', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Editable command rules', caro: true, competitor: true, winner: 'tie' },
    { feature: 'Works in your existing terminal', caro: true, competitor: 'partial', winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'macOS, Linux and Windows', caro: true, competitor: true, winner: 'tie' },
    { feature: 'Full coding agent (edits files, runs tasks)', caro: false, competitor: true, winner: 'competitor' },
    { feature: 'Team collaboration (Drive, session sharing)', caro: false, competitor: true, winner: 'competitor' },
    { feature: 'Modern terminal UI (blocks, tabs)', caro: false, competitor: true, winner: 'competitor' },
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      title: 'What Is Open Source',
      icon: '📖',
      content: `
        <p><strong>Warp</strong> published its client on GitHub on April 28, 2026. Most of the code is AGPL-3.0, and the UI framework crates are MIT. Warp's FAQ says the server, the Warp Drive backend, the Oz orchestration layer and the built-in agent harness are not in the repository. The harness runs on Warp's servers.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> is AGPL-3.0 from end to end: the CLI, the safety patterns, the prompts and the inference code. Its default model, Qwen2.5-Coder 1.5B, is open-weight under Apache-2.0. You can read, audit and change every part that decides which command you see.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Your Terminal',
      icon: '🖥️',
      content: `
        <p><strong>The Warp app</strong> is its own terminal emulator, so you move from iTerm2, Ghostty, Terminal.app or Windows Terminal to Warp. <strong>The Warp Agent CLI</strong>, launched in August 2026, runs inside any terminal and over SSH. It needs a Warp login or API key.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> is a single CLI with optional shell integration for zsh, bash and fish. It does not replace your terminal, your prompt or your config, and it needs no account.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Privacy and Connectivity',
      icon: '🔒',
      content: `
        <p><strong>Warp</strong> sends AI requests through its own backend, including requests that use your own API key or a custom endpoint. Custom endpoints must be public: Warp rejects localhost and private network URLs. Without a network, the terminal works but Warp's AI features do not. On the Free plan, telemetry is on by default and must stay on to use AI. On Business and Enterprise it is off by default. Warp has zero-data-retention agreements with its model providers.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> runs local models on your machine. After a one-time model download, prompts and commands do not leave it, so Caro works on a plane, behind a strict VPN or in an air-gapped network.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Safety Approach',
      icon: '🛡️',
      content: `
        <p><strong>Warp</strong> gives each agent action an autonomy level: Agent decides, Always ask, Always allow or Never. Commands that match a regex denylist need your approval. The default list covers tools like <code>rm</code>, <code>curl</code>, <code>eval</code> and <code>ssh</code>. By default, auto-approve skips your own denylist. Admin denylist rules are never skipped. A match asks; it does not block.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> checks every command against 52+ curated patterns, rates it CRITICAL, HIGH, MEDIUM or LOW, and blocks critical commands such as <code>rm -rf /</code> and fork bombs, whatever the model suggests. You can add your own patterns.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Use Both',
      icon: '🤝',
      content: `
        <p>Caro is a plain CLI, so it runs inside Warp like in any other terminal. Use Warp for its agents and workspace. Use Caro when you want a quick, offline, safety-checked command.</p>
      `
    },
  ],
  verdict: {
    chooseCaro: [
      'You want to keep your terminal and your setup',
      'You need AI that works offline or air-gapped',
      'You do not want an account or telemetry to use AI',
      'You want a curated safety library with risk levels, not only a denylist',
      'You want the whole stack open, inference included',
    ],
    chooseCompetitor: [
      'You want a full agentic development environment',
      'You want hosted frontier models and cloud agents',
      'Team features like Drive and session sharing matter to you',
      'You like a modern terminal UI with blocks and tabs',
    ]
  },
  verification: {
    date: '2026-10-11',
    sources: [
      { label: 'Warp blog: Warp is now open-source (Apr 28, 2026)', url: 'https://www.warp.dev/blog/warp-is-now-open-source' },
      { label: 'warpdotdev/warp repository and FAQ', url: 'https://github.com/warpdotdev/warp' },
      { label: 'Warp docs: custom inference endpoints', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/agents/inference/custom-inference-endpoint/' },
      { label: 'Warp docs: using Warp offline', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/support-and-community/troubleshooting-and-support/using-warp-offline/' },
      { label: 'Warp docs: privacy', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/support-and-community/privacy-and-security/privacy/' },
      { label: 'Warp docs: agent profiles and permissions', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/agents/capabilities/agent-profiles-permissions/' },
      { label: 'Warp blog: Introducing the Warp Agent CLI (Aug 4, 2026)', url: 'https://www.warp.dev/blog/introducing-the-warp-agent-cli-coding-agent' },
      { label: 'Warp docs: installation and setup', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/getting-started/quickstart/installation-and-setup/' },
    ]
  }
};
---

<ComparisonPageLayout
  title="Caro vs Warp - Feature Comparison | Terminal AI Tools"
  description="Compare Caro and Warp. A fully open, offline, safety-checked shell CLI vs an agentic development environment with an open-source client and cloud AI."
  comparison={comparison}
  heroSubtitle="Keep your terminal. Keep your data on your machine."
  finalCtaHeading="Keep Your Terminal. Add AI Safely."
  finalCtaSubtext="Caro runs in the terminal you already use. No account, no cloud required."
/>
